  "executive_briefing": "The global tax and trade landscape in mid-2026 is defined by a deepening divide between U.S. protectionism and the broader global push for harmonized tax structures. The U.S. 'One Big Beautiful Bill Act' has crystallized a domestic tax policy characterized by continuity and isolation from OECD Pillar Two standards, while the administration has accelerated the use of Section 232 authority to implement broad, high-impact tariffs following legal setbacks on IEEPA. Meanwhile, international jurisdictions are demonstrating high agility in mitigating the impact of the 15% global minimum tax through 'sub-headline' fiscal incentives, rebates, and multi-year rate reduction glides. Concurrently, statutory mandates for digital tax compliance\u2014exemplified by Singapore's definitive InvoiceNow roadmap\u2014are effectively ending voluntary adoption, forcing multinationals to modernize tax data infrastructure to meet 2030-2031 mandatory standards.",
